## Roles and Permissions

### Admin

The administrator can:

- View all users
- Change user roles
- Activate/deactivate users
- Access all projects
- Access all tasks

An administrator cannot:

- Remove their own `admin` role
- Deactivate their own account

This prevents the system from being left without an administrator.

### Manager

A manager can:

- Create projects
- View projects they own or belong to
- Update their own projects
- Delete their own projects
- Add members to their projects
- Remove members from their projects
- Create tasks in their projects
- Update/delete tasks in their projects
- Change task status in their projects

A manager cannot modify another manager's project.

### Member

A member can:

- View projects they belong to
- View tasks in projects they belong to
- Change the status of tasks assigned to themselves

A member cannot:

- Create projects
- Manage project membership
- Modify arbitrary task information
- Change the status of a task assigned to another user

---

## Authentication

### Registration

```http
POST /auth/register
```

Example:

```json
{
  "email": "user@example.com",
  "password": "password123",
  "full_name": "Example User"
}
```

New users receive the `member` role by default.

### Login

```http
POST /auth/login
```

Example:

```json
{
  "email": "user@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}
```

Successful authentication returns:

```json
{
  "access_token": "...",
  "refresh_token": "...",
  "token_type": "bearer"
}
```

The access token is used with:

```http
Authorization: Bearer <access_token>
```

### Refresh

```http
POST /auth/refresh
```

A refresh token is exchanged for a new access/refresh token pair.

The previous refresh token is revoked during rotation.

### Logout

```http
POST /auth/logout
```

The supplied refresh token is revoked.

### Current user

```http
GET /auth/me
```

Requires a valid access token.

### Change password

```http
PUT /auth/me/password
```

Example:

```json
{
  "current_password": "old-password",
  "new_password": "new-password"
}
```

The new password must contain between 8 and 128 characters.

After a successful password change, all active refresh tokens belonging to the user are revoked.

---

## First Admin

The assignment requires the first administrator to be created through a non-public mechanism rather than through the public registration endpoint.

The public registration endpoint therefore always creates a `member`.

**Project-specific first-admin setup:**  
`[Document the exact seed/environment-variable command used by this project here.]`

The important security property is that a normal user cannot register through `/auth/register` and obtain the `admin` role.

---

## Authorization

Authorization is centralized in:

```text
app/api/dependencies.py
```

Important authorization dependencies include:

```text
get_current_user
require_roles
require_project_member
require_project_owner
require_task_access
```

For example:

```text
require_roles("admin")
```

restricts an endpoint to administrators.

Resource-specific dependencies then verify ownership or membership.

This avoids repeating authorization logic in every route handler.

---

## Database

The project uses SQLite with SQLAlchemy.

The database contains the following main entities:

```text
User
Project
ProjectMember
Task
RefreshToken
LoginAttempt
```

### User

```text
id
email
password_hash
full_name
role
is_active
created_at
```

### User

```text
id
email
ip_address
attempted_at
```

### Project

```text
id
name
description
owner_id
created_at
```

### ProjectMember

```text
project_id
user_id
```

### Task

```text
id
project_id
title
description
status
assignee_id
created_by
created_at
updated_at
```

Supported task statuses:

```text
todo
in_progress
done
```

### RefreshToken

Refresh tokens are stored as hashes rather than plaintext token values.

---

## Database Migrations

Alembic is used for schema migrations.

Apply all migrations with:

```bash
alembic upgrade head
```

Check the current migration:

```bash
alembic current
```

The SQLite database file is intentionally excluded from Git.

A fresh database can therefore be created from the migration history instead of relying on a developer's local database file.

